  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nOne wave of showers moved through this morning and is currently\npushing out of the Arrowhead. Meanwhile, the second wave of\nshowers, and a few thunderstorms, has developed from near\nBrainerd eastward across the I-35 corridor, just behind a\nlifting warm front. Cannot rule out a few thunderstorms as temps\ncool aloft, with 250 J/kg of MUCAPE and a 40-50kts of bulk\nshear. Do not expect any severe storms. Rain showers and those\nfew thunderstorms will continue to lift to the east northeast,\nas a cold front approaches from the west. Total rainfall amounts\nwill be around 0.25 inch, with slightly higher amounts possible\nif you are under one of the thunderstorms. \n\nCAM guidance is in pretty good agreement that drier air arrives\nfrom the west this evening, with rainfall moving out of the \narea by 7-10PM from west to east. Gusty south to southwest \nwinds will turn to the northwest as the cold front passes, and\nremain gusty through tomorrow. Gusts as high as 30mph are\npossible over the Arrowhead tomorrow. Elsewhere, looking at\ngusts of 20-25mph. High pressure arrives Sunday evening, with \nclear skies and diminishing winds. This will allow temperatures \nto fall below freezing for portions of north central MN, the \nArrowhead, and inland northwest WI. Elsewhere, frost is \npossible, and headlines will be needed for Sunday night into \nMonday morning. \n\nSoutherly winds return on Monday as high pressure shifts to the\neast. Abundant sunshine and a warming trend will push afternoon\nhigh temperatures into the upper 60s and lower 70s for Tuesday.\nRipples in northwesterly flow aloft will be of little \nconsequence through the end of the week, as broad upper level \nridging across the southern CONUS keeps moisture trapped well to\nour south. Other than increases to cloud cover at times, a weak\ntrough passing around mid- week will bring slightly cooler (but\nstill above average) air toward the end of the week.",
